@page "/draft-load"

<PageTitle>Loading a saved draft</PageTitle>
<h1>Loading a saved draft</h1>
<p>A form filled from a saved draft looks untouched until the page asks the form what the loaded values have earned. One call answers three ways: a good value turns green, a wrong value shows its message, and a field the draft left blank stays quiet.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "SavedProposal.cs", "DraftLoad.razor", "DraftLoad.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the form before touching anything.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> three empty boxes, each with an asterisk, and no borders or messages.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Load saved draft</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Title</strong> turns green, <strong>Contact email</strong> turns red with "That is not a valid email address", and <strong>Summary</strong> stays as it was.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> After filling the model, the button calls <code>DiscloseLoadedValuesAsync()</code> to check the loaded values.</div></li>
            <li>Look at <strong>Summary</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> it is still marked required, and still has no message.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> <code>DiscloseLoadedValuesAsync()</code> leaves an empty field alone, even one whose required rule fails.</div></li>
            <li>Look at the message under <strong>Contact email</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "That is not a valid email address", the address-format rule's message, not the required rule's "A contact email is required".</div></li>
            <li>Select everything in <strong>Contact email</strong>, type <code>ada@example.com</code>, and press Tab.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the message clears and the border turns green.</div></li>
            <li>Type anything into <strong>Summary</strong> and press Tab.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> <strong>Summary</strong> turns green.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted" in the status line.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Start blank</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the boxes empty, and all three green borders clear at once.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Load saved draft</strong> again.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the same three answers as in step 2.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong>: required; a valid address once the box holds anything.</li>
            <li><strong>Summary</strong>: required.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>Writing values into a model notifies nothing, so a form filled from a saved draft looks untouched: no border and no message, however good or bad the values. Unless the page asks the form about the loaded values, every border and every message waits for the visitor to change something or press <strong>Submit</strong>.</p>
        <p><strong>Load saved draft</strong> fills the model, then calls the form's <code>DiscloseLoadedValuesAsync()</code>. That call checks the whole model against the rules a submit runs, then answers field by field on whether the field holds a value. A value that passes turns green; a value that fails shows its message; a field holding nothing is left alone.</p>
        <p>The saved draft holds one of each. <strong>Title</strong> has a good value, <strong>Contact email</strong> a wrong one, and <strong>Summary</strong> none, so it is a field nobody has reached yet.</p>
        <p><strong>Contact email</strong> has two rules: one requires a value, and one checks the address's format. The saved value passes the first and fails the second. A failed format rule is what marks the field as "this one is wrong" rather than "you have not got to this yet".</p>
        <p>The call treats each field holding a value as if you had changed it. Marking a field only as touched (visited, with no change) would turn the good fields green but leave the wrong value silent among them, which reads as "not filled in yet" rather than "this is wrong".</p>
        <p>The asterisks come from the same validator, through <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code> (the component that draws the mark). A required field can be marked and silent at the same time, and that is the point: the mark says the value is needed, the silence says nothing is nagging you about a box you have not opened yet.</p>
        <p>"Holds a value" means exactly what <code>NotEmpty()</code> means for the property's declared type. A blank or whitespace-only string holds nothing; so does an empty list, and so does a non-nullable <code>int</code>, <code>bool</code> or <code>DateTime</code> at its default. A <code>bool?</code> holding <code>false</code> is a real saved answer and earns its border, which is why an optional value is worth declaring as <code>T?</code>.</p>
        <p>The call says nothing about a value it cannot read (on a nested path whose owner is null) or about a model-level rule, which names no field. Turning a good value green needs the validator to list the fields it has rules for. A validator that cannot list its rules still turns a wrong value red but turns nothing green.</p>
        <p>A value the model's own constructor seeded is still a value: seed a placeholder your rules reject, and the form rejects it the moment the draft loads.</p>
        <p>A box with focus wears the blue focus border, whatever its state, so <strong>Contact email</strong> turns green only once you leave it. <strong>Summary</strong> turns green the ordinary way, when you change it. The load gave <strong>Title</strong> its green border and <strong>Contact email</strong> its red one without a keystroke.</p>
        <p><strong>Start blank</strong> empties the three model properties itself, then calls the form's <code>ResetAsync()</code>, which clears every border and message but never writes a model property. No transition delays the borders, so all three clear at once, and the form looks untouched again.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_proposal" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary />
            </div>

            <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _proposal.Title" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_proposal.Title" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _proposal.Title" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Contact email <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _proposal.ContactEmail" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_proposal.ContactEmail" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _proposal.ContactEmail" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Summary <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _proposal.Summary" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_proposal.Summary" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _proposal.Summary" /></div>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit">Submit</button>
                <button type="button" @onclick="LoadSavedDraft">Load saved draft</button>
                <button type="button" @onclick="StartBlank">Start blank</button>
            </div>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
